\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Urban Wetland Buffers and Stormwater Retention:\\ Evidence from a Paired-Catchment Study}
\author{K.~Agyeman and L.~Moreau}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Urban wetlands are increasingly promoted as nature-based
solutions for stormwater management, yet catchment-scale
evidence for their retention performance remains thin.
\cite{smith2020} report attenuation of peak flows downstream
of restored wetland buffers, and their design has become a
reference point for municipal programmes.

\section{Study Design}
We instrumented two adjacent urban catchments that differ
only in the presence of a restored wetland buffer and
monitored discharge through two full storm seasons.
Following \cite{smith2020}, retention was quantified as the
reduction in peak discharge relative to the control
catchment for matched storm events.

\section{Results}
Across twenty-two matched events, the wetland catchment
showed lower peaks and longer recessions than the control.
The retention effect grew with storm magnitude up to the
design event and declined for the largest storms,
a pattern consistent with the bypass behaviour described
by \cite{smith2020}.

\section{Discussion}
The catchment-scale retention we observe complements the
laboratory sediment measurements of \cite{smith2020b}:
together they indicate that restored buffers influence both
the timing of discharge and the export of suspended solids.
Design guidance should therefore treat hydrologic and
water-quality benefits as coupled outcomes.

\section{Conclusion}
Paired-catchment monitoring confirms that a restored urban
wetland buffer measurably retards stormflow, with the
largest relative benefits in moderate events.

